WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.670
There's something undeniably entertaining
about watching actors mess up a scene by flubbing

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:08.000
a line, breaking character, or even walking
into a wall.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.330
These classic movie bloopers are just as entertaining
as the films they came from — in fact, in

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:16.400
some cases, they're even better — and they've
kept us cracking up for years.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.369
Smokey and the Bandit 2

00:00:18.369 --> 00:00:23.300
More than 35 years after the release of Smokey
and the Bandit 2, many comedies still run

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.460
a blooper reel over the closing credits.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:29.569
And from Bandits' credits, it's clear that
stars Burt Reynolds and Sally Field had a

00:00:29.569 --> 00:00:31.229
blast working on the film.

00:00:31.229 --> 00:00:32.340
“When do we get started?”

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.040
“That’s my line, you say ‘whenever you
like.’”

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:35.860
“Whenever you like.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:37.320
It’s alright, keep it rolling, this is good
stuff.”

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:38.380
“Yeah, I like this."

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:43.039
Sadly, in the years since, a credits blooper
reel has actually become a pretty reliable

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.060
indicator that the movie itself isn't all
that funny.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.520
“You’re wrong.”

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:54.400
Home Alone

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.559
Unless you're some kind of monster, there's
not much that's funny about watching a child

00:00:58.559 --> 00:00:59.559
get hurt.

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:04.030
But when Macaulay Culkin slips on ice in an
outtake from the first Home Alone film...well,

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.640
it's downright hilarious.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:11.140
“I thought the Murphys went to…”

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:17.180
Perhaps it's because falling down is always
funny — no matter who's doing the falling.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:18.690
North by Northwest

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:23.710
In one of Alfred Hitchcock's best films, North
by Northwest, Eva Marie Saint's character

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:25.830
pulls out a gun in a crowded cafeteria.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:30.280
As suspenseful as it is, viewers who pay close
attention will know ahead of time that she's

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.110
going to fire that gun.

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:36.960
That's all thanks to a little boy in the background
who plugs his ears five seconds too early.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.310
“Just get back.”

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:41.700
“Why you little fool.”

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.180
“You just stay away from me.”

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.220
[Gunshot and screaming]

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:47.460
Back to the Future

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:52.350
In this classic time travel comedy, Michael
J. Fox's character, Marty, takes a swig from

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:56.210
a bottle of booze while on a date with Lorraine,
played by Lea Thompson.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:02:01.060
To his surprise, some Back to the Future associates
decided to help him out...by filling it with

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:01.880
actual liquor.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.140
[Laughter]

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.760
His reaction was 100% real...and he didn't
seem too upset about it, either.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:18.440
The Frighteners

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:22.940
Before he moved on to epic films like The
Lord of the Rings and King Kong, Peter Jackson

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:27.230
directed this quirky horror movie about a
guy who develops the ability to communicate

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:28.360
with ghosts.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:32.750
Though Michael J. Fox wanted audiences to
associate him with something other than Back

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.660
to the Future, it seems that even he had a
hard time doing that himself.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:40.850
During the shoot, when speaking to the character
named "The Judge," Fox kept calling him "Doc"

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:43.020
— as in "Doc Brown" from Back to the Future.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:46.320
“Little cook in the Pony Express, huh Doc?

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:47.320
Whoa!

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:48.390
I called him Doc.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.360
It's f---ing Back to the Future."

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:53.060
“Doc!

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:56.720
Doc, I did it again.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.520
Wrong f---in’ movie."

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:01.020
We're the Millers

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:05.990
Jason Sudeikis Jennifer Aniston, Emma Roberts,
and Will Poulter pretend to be a family as

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:08.690
part of a drug smuggling operation in We're
the Millers.

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:13.960
In one scene, Sudeikis's character turns on
a radio, and TLC's "Waterfalls" is supposed

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:14.960
to play.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:19.530
Instead, the cast and crew took this opportunity
to prank Aniston — by blasting the theme

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:21.300
from Friends instead.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.920
“I love this song!”

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:27.040
“Woo!”

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:34.940
Aniston's emotional reaction made it totally
worth it.

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:37.740
“That was really good.”

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.410
Liar Liar

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:46.660
Jim Carrey is an incredible improviser, and
directors will often just keep the cameras

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:48.630
rolling and let him experiment.

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:53.319
Liar Liar is one of Carrey's most over-the-top
comedic performances, and while the filmmakers

00:03:53.319 --> 00:03:57.490
couldn't use all of his hilarious improv in
the film, they created a gag reel to play

00:03:57.490 --> 00:03:59.540
over the credits.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:02.960
"Prenuptial agreements."

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:08.400
[Laughter]

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:12.160
It's also quite a treat to watch cast members
clap back at him with hilarious moments of

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:13.120
their own.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:14.360
“Your honor, I object!"

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:15.160
“You would!”

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:16.280
“Over-actor!”

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:17.900
“Jazz-”
[Laughter]

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:22.560
Star Trek

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:26.560
One thing unites the many different Star Trek
properties, from the original series in the

00:04:26.569 --> 00:04:31.860
1960s, to the world-expanding shows in the
'80s and '90s, to the big-budget film reboots

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:37.979
of the 21st century: Spaceship doors never
seem to work correctly.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.639
“Sensors are configured-”
"Aw, c'mon!"

00:04:42.639 --> 00:04:48.210
For generations, these characters just can't
seem to exit a room.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:49.470
“Very-”

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:51.080
Raiders of the Lost Ark

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:55.330
The classic Indiana Jones flick Raiders of
the Lost Ark is a film with a mistake that

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:59.400
actually made it into the final cut — and
it's kind of a gross one, so of course we

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:00.400
can't look away.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:04.960
In an extreme close-up on actor Paul Freeman,
the camera catches a fly landing on his face

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:07.160
and crawling right into his mouth.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:10.219
"Your persistence surprises even me.

00:05:10.219 --> 00:05:14.150
You’re gonna give mercenaries a bad name."

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:18.870
In an interview with fan-site The Indy Experience,
Freeman claimed that the fly never actually

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:23.379
went into his mouth, but that an editor played
around with frames to make it look like it

00:05:23.379 --> 00:05:24.379
did.

00:05:24.379 --> 00:05:28.539
The effect is gross enough that there's really
only one appropriate reaction:

00:05:28.539 --> 00:05:30.779
[Screaming]

00:05:30.779 --> 00:05:36.809
Meanwhile, another beloved movie has a blooper
that made its way into the final cut — and

00:05:36.809 --> 00:05:41.169
is now as cherished a part of the movie as
any of its other parts.

00:05:41.169 --> 00:05:42.169
Star Wars

00:05:42.169 --> 00:05:46.419
Probably because it's part of one of the most
successful and beloved movies of all time,

00:05:46.419 --> 00:05:50.759
the stormtrooper bonking his head on a doorframe
during Star Wars is one of the most famous

00:05:50.759 --> 00:05:54.050
movie bloopers ever — and it never gets
old.

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:56.069
“Take over.”

00:05:56.069 --> 00:06:00.419
It's a rare and welcome bit of physical comedy
in the otherwise dramatic space opera.

00:06:00.419 --> 00:06:05.330
Plus, with stormtroopers constantly getting
themselves shot, blown up, and thrown around,

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:09.599
this minor mishap really helps drive home
the idea that these faceless soldiers are

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:12.340
the Imperial Keystone Cops of outer space.

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:13.340
"Huh?"

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:14.660
Thanks for watching!

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.659
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:17.659 --> 00:06:19.830
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

